From: Yury Klushin <digger@live.ru>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 14:26:16 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] Make the assembler write ELF for pdp11-*-rt11*

gas already spoke BFD_RELOC_* codes and already had a tc_gen_reloc, so
most of this is choosing the format and then saying what the addends
mean.

The relocations are RELA, so md_apply_fix stops writing the value into
the field whenever a relocation is going to be emitted -- the addend
carries all of it -- and tc_gen_reloc builds that addend from
fx_offset.  For a PC-relative fixup it subtracts the operand size,
because the machine reaches such an operand from the word after the one
holding the displacement while ELF measures from that word itself; that
is the -2 include/elf/pdp11.h describes.

Sections get an even alignment.  a.out sections carry 2**1 because the
format has no way to say anything else, but an ELF section is whatever
it says it is, and one left at 2**0 can be placed on an odd address by
the linker.  md_begin raises the three standard sections and
md_elf_section_change_hook raises every section a directive creates, so
.text.foo and the rest are covered when they start appearing.

Four of the five gas tests here check what was left in the field of a
relocated operand, which is a property of the object format rather than
of the assembler's decoding, so they get an ELF expectation file each
and pdp11.exp picks by format.  is_elf_format learns that the rt11 OS
is ELF (a plain pdp11-*-aout target is not).  All five pass.

Checked against the assembler this replaces, on 36 translation units --
every example, test and library source in the uknc project, compiled to
assembly and then assembled both ways.  Every one has identical section
sizes and identical contents at every byte not covered by a relocation,
which is the whole instruction stream.  29 of the 36 have identical
relocations as well; in the other seven ELF keeps a relocation where
a.out folded the value in, and every symbol involved is .globl -- which
is what ELF is supposed to do, since the linker has to be able to bind a
global symbol to a definition elsewhere.

Note that this on its own leaves the toolchain unable to link: ld still
produces a.out for this target and will not read what gas now writes.
That is the next patch.

Includes what was a separate fix on top of this one, now part of it:

Stop padding sections the machine does not load

md_section_align rounded every section up to an even size.  That is the
right rule for a section the machine loads -- its own alignment is two
-- and the wrong one for a section a tool merely reads.  The debug
sections and the streams -flto leaves behind are byte streams whose
length is part of their meaning, and a zero byte on the end of one is
not padding but corruption: a .gnu.lto_ section of 11 bytes came out 12
long, and -flto failed with "compressed stream: buffer error", zlib
having been handed a byte past the end of its stream.

Round up only what is allocated.  The section-change hook gets the same
treatment, for the same reason -- there is nothing to align in a section
that is never at an address.

Includes what was a separate fix on top of this one, now part of it:

Clear a relocated field instead of leaving it

With RELA the addend carries the value and md_apply_fix writes nothing,
which left whatever was already in the word behind a relocatable
operand -- and the assembler does not always initialise that.  Where the
a.out path overwrote it every time, here it survived into the object,
so two builds of the same source could differ.  Seven of this project's
object files did.

Nothing was ever wrong with the programs: the linker ignores the field
under a RELA relocation, and every .sav and .ppu came out identical
throughout.  But an object file ought not to depend on what was last in
that memory.  Clear the field, and only the field -- an eight-bit branch
displacement shares its word with the opcode.

Two runs of the toolchain now produce all 34 artifacts of this project's
examples and tests byte for byte alike.
